            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Philosophical Evolution</strong><br>\nAfter a pilgrimage to Mecca in 1964, Malcolm X's views evolved to embrace a more inclusive approach to the fight for equality. He began to see the possibility of cooperation between races and spoke against the Nation of Islam's separatist ideology. This shift marked a significant change in his philosophy, reflecting a more universalist view of the struggle for Black rights.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Founding of Muslim Mosque, Inc.</strong><br>\nAfter leaving the Nation of Islam, Malcolm X founded Muslim Mosque, Inc., which aimed to internationalize the plight of African Americans and connect their struggle with that of oppressed peoples worldwide. He sought to unify African Americans through the Islamic faith and cultural identity, advocating for their right to self-determination.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Founding of the Organization of Afro-American Unity</strong><br>\nIn addition to his religious efforts, Malcolm X also established the Organization of Afro-American Unity (OAAU), modeled after the Organization of African Unity. The OAAU was secular and focused on fighting for human rights for African Americans and promoting cooperation among all people of African descent.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
