          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Assassination and Impact</strong><br>\nOn February 21, 1965, Malcolm X was assassinated while addressing his organization in Harlem. His death was a major blow to the civil rights movement but served to immortalize his ideas and influence future generations. Malcolm X's legacy is reflected in the ongoing struggles for justice and equality.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Malcolm X's International Influence</strong><br>\nMalcolm X is remembered for extending the American civil rights movement to the global stage. He connected the African American freedom struggle with liberation movements in Africa, Asia, and Latin America, influencing numerous leaders and activists. His efforts to link civil rights to human rights broadened the scope of the struggle for equality.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Remembering Malcolm X</strong><br>\nToday, Malcolm X is celebrated as one of the most influential African Americans in history. His life's work continues to inspire people fighting against oppression. Schools and streets bear his name, and his autobiography remains a crucial text for understanding the civil rights era and the complex history of race in America.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
